#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718919 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(- 6 y^{2} x^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(a x - 4 y x^{3}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 12 y x^{2} \\\\ Q_x&=a - 12 y x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality must hold for every point of the plane. Simplifying this identity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a&=0 \\\\ a&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe matching terms cancel as identities. Any common factor that vanishes at some points is nonzero elsewhere; equality at those zeros alone would not determine the constant.\n\nAt $a=0$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718924 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(6 x^{2} + \\cos{\\left(4 \\pi y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(a x - 4 \\pi x \\sin{\\left(4 \\pi y \\right)} + 3 x + 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- 4 \\pi \\sin{\\left(4 \\pi y \\right)} \\\\ Q_x&=a - 4 \\pi \\sin{\\left(4 \\pi y \\right)} + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality must hold for every point of the plane. Simplifying this identity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a + 3&=0 \\\\ a&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe matching terms cancel as identities. Any common factor that vanishes at some points is nonzero elsewhere; equality at those zeros alone would not determine the constant.\n\nAt $a=-3$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718925 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(9 y x^{2} + 2 y + 3 x^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(- a x + 3 y^{2} + 3 x^{3}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=9 x^{2} + 2 \\\\ Q_x&=- a + 9 x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality must hold for every point of the plane. Simplifying this identity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- a - 2&=0 \\\\ a&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe matching terms cancel as identities. Any common factor that vanishes at some points is nonzero elsewhere; equality at those zeros alone would not determine the constant.\n\nAt $a=-2$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718938 :question/problem "![](images/db/db40e0220317d76c618a495952b74be0fb9b69b7fd5aa07e0e7e1db07f6fca3e.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y,- x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Let $C$ be one-half of this ellipse, traversed clockwise from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(0,-1)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/8d/8dd16a504be78e59976221c87c3ff9366ae1327ab4a2c01611fdfa9291f7c5fd.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c6/c69ae349d559b26523d311c7e2bfb7b489084d689368b734ee93389f147499aa.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718945 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a789e75d4ba819064a3364d76de1d5b0a9ae7890c9592c70301a72e2c51741b.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y,- 4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(-1,0)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points against the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore negative.\n\n![](images/c6/c64c8ccfa7f272ab00b1ff8bfe2389402a833863766075ba49679fbd5376cbb7.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/1a/1ab43e6773bc938b65ad94064e442956e8af6408aaebc3014b8108a24773047c.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718966 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac30aeac69da8e1f91fd538e4a89b090a96175753ad7435f0c22971fc8c3f681.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 9 y,4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be one-half of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-2)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/08/0835be72be55a36cf4357cb7b5b82e58308e79d7481902eeac919a1ca64d637f.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c4f497d0d97e1f37b6a4c291207c2c3dc140646cc840c76556590cd11248651.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718974 :question/problem "![](images/95/9505a6578e13c299c59a790672e0db3420aeeaecee7ec1dc169a6407e464e131.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 9 y,4 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed clockwise from $A=(-3,0)$ to $B=(0,-2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points against the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore negative.\n\n![](images/43/43fc678ed01a8d6bd7e709c4138e9355e957ed0d3a295d767bfb68a5e4a4bd81.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/b8/b802348ea9bb3cc286faf1bbabf8c409c4793590ad86c55783071d61238d1519.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718977 :question/problem "![](images/14/1446c8eb7bc22596616d757b5f403d9364e1d69147dfd1a58656a38266df16ff.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 4 y,9 x\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$. Let $C$ be three-quarters of this ellipse, traversed counterclockwise from $A=(0,-3)$ to $B=(-2,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field is tangent to the ellipse. Along the indicated orientation, the field points with the motion at every point. Every contribution is therefore positive.\n\n![](images/70/7006fa694d06d062ad4b5596745fdea74bdc7f76ec409a8bdc1ac81c78af8a4b.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/53/53c9dd47332ff72759429d5599687efe24ece88c7eebf02c06cfbffe52a24d5c.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718992 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(9 y^{2} x + 25 y + x^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(a^{2} y x^{2} + y^{2} + x \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=18 y x + 25 \\\\ Q_x&=2 a^{2} y x + \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality is an identity in $x$ and $y$, so the coefficient of $xy$ and the constant term must both agree:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^2&=9 \\\\ \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}&=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe first condition gives $a=-3$ or $a=3$. The second gives $a=3$ or $a=-7$. Their only common value is $a=3$. The opposite root of the first condition fails the constant-term condition; the other root of the second fails the coefficient condition.\n\nAt $a=3$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719002 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a^{2} x y^{2} - x^{2} + y \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(16 x^{2} y + 4 x - y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a^{2} x y + \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\ Q_x&=32 x y + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality is an identity in $x$ and $y$, so the coefficient of $xy$ and the constant term must both agree:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^2&=16 \\\\ \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}&=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe first condition gives $a=-4$ or $a=4$. The second gives $a=-4$ or $a=0$. Their only common value is $a=-4$. The opposite root of the first condition fails the constant-term condition; the other root of the second fails the coefficient condition.\n\nAt $a=-4$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719003 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a^{2} x y^{2} + y \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(16 x^{2} y + 36 x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a^{2} x y + \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\ Q_x&=32 x y + 36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality is an identity in $x$ and $y$, so the coefficient of $xy$ and the constant term must both agree:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^2&=16 \\\\ \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}&=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe first condition gives $a=-4$ or $a=4$. The second gives $a=4$ or $a=-8$. Their only common value is $a=4$. The opposite root of the first condition fails the constant-term condition; the other root of the second fails the coefficient condition.\n\nAt $a=4$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719008 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a^{2} x y^{2} + y \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(9 x^{2} y + 25 x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a^{2} x y + \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\ Q_x&=18 x y + 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality is an identity in $x$ and $y$, so the coefficient of $xy$ and the constant term must both agree:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^2&=9 \\\\ \\left(a + 2\\right)^{2}&=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe first condition gives $a=-3$ or $a=3$. The second gives $a=3$ or $a=-7$. Their only common value is $a=3$. The opposite root of the first condition fails the constant-term condition; the other root of the second fails the coefficient condition.\n\nAt $a=3$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719054 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(9 y^{2} x - x^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(4 a^{2} y x^{2} - y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a negative constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=18 y x \\\\ Q_x&=8 a^{2} y x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative equality must hold everywhere, including points where $xy\\ne0$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 a^{2}&=9 \\\\ a^2&=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ a&=- \\frac{3}{2}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe prompt requires $a$ to be negative, so $a=- \\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nAt $a=- \\frac{3}{2}$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719057 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a^{2} x y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(25 x^{2} y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a positive constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a^{2} x y \\\\ Q_x&=50 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative equality must hold everywhere, including points where $xy\\ne0$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^{2}&=25 \\\\ a^2&=25 \\\\ a&=-5\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe prompt requires $a$ to be positive, so $a=5$.\n\nAt $a=5$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719058 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a^{2} x y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(9 x^{2} y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a negative constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a^{2} x y \\\\ Q_x&=18 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative equality must hold everywhere, including points where $xy\\ne0$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a^{2}&=9 \\\\ a^2&=9 \\\\ a&=-3\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe prompt requires $a$ to be negative, so $a=-3$.\n\nAt $a=-3$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719063 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(4 a^{2} x y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(9 x^{2} y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a negative constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=8 a^{2} x y \\\\ Q_x&=18 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative equality must hold everywhere, including points where $xy\\ne0$. Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 a^{2}&=9 \\\\ a^2&=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ a&=- \\frac{3}{2}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe prompt requires $a$ to be negative, so $a=- \\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nAt $a=- \\frac{3}{2}$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719071 :question/problem "![](images/72/72508c4a3efb65116b33e33aabf582ba521f724139985e67130244c2abda1680.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(0,- y - 3\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $y>0$ from $A=(2,0)$ to $B=(-2,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points downward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $y=v$, where $v>0$. Their $x$ coordinates are $u$ and $- u$, where $u>0$. Their small $y$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{y,1}-F_{y,2}&=\\left(- v - 3\\right)-\\left(- v - 3\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe paired forces are equal. Equal opposite displacements therefore give equal opposite work. Every pair cancels, giving zero total work. This is cancellation, rather than perpendicularity everywhere.\n\n![](images/1c/1c78101232dbb9ac923334b258fe54359aa0d93003f8ac31da7359b9deeb195e.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c6/c668801541df26bccc1da55da266e408e71c35b92ccf7cb717e5a6cd0831331e.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719087 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d815ef57c70cb5009946ae5a15a6b942a414ea738e558018b8d8ecbcf604dd03.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x - y + 2,0\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $x>0$ from $A=(0,-1)$ to $B=(0,1)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points rightward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does positive work; on the second half it does negative work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $x=v$, where $v>0$. Their $y$ coordinates are $- u$ and $u$, where $u>0$. Their small $x$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{x,1}-F_{x,2}&=\\left(u + v + 2\\right)-\\left(- u + v + 2\\right) \\\\ &=2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first displacement in $x$ is positive. Multiplying it by the displayed force difference gives positive work for every pair. The positive contribution dominates, so the total work has that sign. Equal endpoint coordinates alone would not establish cancellation.\n\n![](images/cd/cd381c45a7e01660b0cb794ff6d1027aad66d47fc3abfd1fa6b8b9fe5c4f4a03.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/c0/c04e8c91f724439e8609217aba632f47c37872edb396dc88ca52686807400e82.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719088 :question/problem "![](images/56/56bf08ccda891beb79635a79b89a9e503d1a828dd4e8746bb9f35bd3216ea3ed.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(0,- y - 2\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $y>0$ from $A=(1,0)$ to $B=(-1,0)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points downward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $y=v$, where $v>0$. Their $x$ coordinates are $u$ and $- u$, where $u>0$. Their small $y$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{y,1}-F_{y,2}&=\\left(- v - 2\\right)-\\left(- v - 2\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe paired forces are equal. Equal opposite displacements therefore give equal opposite work. Every pair cancels, giving zero total work. This is cancellation, rather than perpendicularity everywhere.\n\n![](images/a5/a58e221c447746fbcc6f28cbdb62d692850e85250cd934ff2b03d90e28760a98.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/4e/4e48c79f38105eb8189b96bcd42b82b49f4bb3284dcd4f65c44f0e3e557d5ba9.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719098 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d5e961f47eb897419a1de496c142dee2d4f2bac56547cf9a2eda6ad73f53e07.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x - y + 3,0\\right)$. The blue path lies on $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Let $C$ be the half of this ellipse in $x<0$ from $A=(0,-2)$ to $B=(0,2)$. Let $-C$ denote the same path with the orientation reversed.\n\nWhich statements are true? **Select all that apply.**" :question/worked-solution "The line integral measures signed work: assistance contributes positive work, opposition negative work, and perpendicular force zero work.\n\nThe field points rightward everywhere on this arc. On the first half it does negative work; on the second half it does positive work. The work parallel to the other coordinate axis is zero.\n\nPair the point on the first half with its reflection on the second half at the same $x=- v$, where $v>0$. Their $y$ coordinates are $- u$ and $u$, where $u>0$. Their small $x$ displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. The signed field components at the paired points differ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_{x,1}-F_{x,2}&=\\left(u + v + 3\\right)-\\left(- u + v + 3\\right) \\\\ &=2 u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first displacement in $x$ is negative. Multiplying it by the displayed force difference gives negative work for every pair. The negative contribution dominates, so the total work has that sign. Equal endpoint coordinates alone would not establish cancellation.\n\n![](images/dc/dcbe02c44ca96053446a8e9d223f9f0aaff6fb2f142406c10bbeb67042b44f3e.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$. Reversing the path reverses every displacement while keeping the field fixed:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n![](images/8c/8cef4d6e4825021b497faa489b483465877a68792e5770dd22aba7ffa0433e42.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$.\n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, is **true**.\n\nStatement 2, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, is **false**.\n\nStatement 3, $\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$, is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719112 :question/problem "![](images/77/77419eacd4ad3c33252b635ad4763ab21c5a2c9072b18f098340efead5a1e12e.png)\n\nFind the area of $\\triangle ABC$, in square units. Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The sides $CA=9$ and $CB=13$ meet at $C$, so $37^{\\circ}$ is their included angle. Use\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A=\\frac12(CA)(CB)\\sin C.\n$$\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(9)(13)\\sin 37^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=58.5\\sin 37^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=35.20617885\\ldots\\\\\n&\\approx35.21.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $35.21$ square units. Keep the calculator's full precision until the final rounding."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719114 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f5c8eff55cd477831f43d3d8508ca76c4f428dba474c1f68b84ff76b571ff10.png)\n\n$E$ lies on $BC$. The two marked angles at $A$ are $\\angle BAE=24^{\\circ}$ and $\\angle EAC=30^{\\circ}$.\n\nRounded to **two decimal places**, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is {{field-1}} square units.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "The included angle between $AB=8$ and $AC=11$ is the whole angle $\\angle BAC$, not either marked part. Add the adjacent angles:\n\n$$\n\\angle BAC=24^{\\circ}+30^{\\circ}=54^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nUse these two sides and their included angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(AB)(AC)\\sin(\\angle BAC)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(8)(11)\\sin54^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=44\\sin54^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=35.59674775\\ldots\\\\\n&\\approx35.60.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $35.60$ square units. Using only $24^{\\circ}$ or $30^{\\circ}$ with sides $AB$ and $AC$ would pair those sides with the wrong angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719118 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6caafe3e5db48af9acf0fe404d10eadc112fb1b9dc4e3b9a62aafad234b7fef2.png)\n\n$E$ lies on $AB$. The two marked angles at $C$ are $\\angle BCE=28^{\\circ}$ and $\\angle ECA=40^{\\circ}$. The side lengths are $CA=x+2$ and $CB=2x$, and $CA+CB=23$. All lengths use the same unit.\n\nRounded to **two decimal places**, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is {{field-1}} square units.\n\n> Use a scientific calculator in degree mode." :question/worked-solution "First determine the two side lengths. Their sum is $23$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x+2)+2x&=23\\\\\n3x+2&=23\\\\\nx&=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $CA=x+2=9$ and $CB=2x=14$.\n\nThe included angle between these sides is the whole angle $\\angle ACB$. Add its two marked parts:\n\n$$\n\\angle ACB=28^{\\circ}+40^{\\circ}=68^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nNow apply the triangle area formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A&=\\frac12(CA)(CB)\\sin(\\angle ACB)\\\\\n&=\\frac12(9)(14)\\sin68^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=63\\sin68^{\\circ}\\\\\n&=58.41258283\\ldots\\\\\n&\\approx58.41.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area is $58.41$ square units. Use the side lengths $9$ and $14$, rather than $x$ itself. Both the side expressions and the two angle markings must be combined before substituting into the formula."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719127 :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3d47e0d43882ed83e4ea0fd7a137c4be00fda055217afede84a140bf347d671.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ follows the semicircular arc and then its diameter, once in the direction of the arrows. The circular arc has radius $4$ and center $(0,1)$. The straight part joins $A(-4,1)$, $B(4,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The **line integral of unity** is the total path length. Split the path into its 2 pieces:\n\n![](images/09/0925e2d997ce37c69d51c7a5c58f30a335b6fb1501d6d852efc447b106aa70e3.png)\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2.\n$$\n\nThe circular piece contributes\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac12(2\\pi\\cdot 4)=4 \\pi.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula for the straight pieces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(4-(-4))^2+(1-(1))^2}=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd every traversed length positively:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=4 \\pi+8=8 + 4 \\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719140 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9cb52534c444da338247543927c7b2efad546fd9dfa5ecae635e83768e16c3.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ follows the semicircular arc and then its diameter, once in the direction of the arrows. The circular arc has radius $7$ and center $(0,1)$. The straight part joins $A(-7,1)$, $B(7,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The **line integral of unity** is the total path length. Split the path into its 2 pieces:\n\n![](images/be/be822384fab36cd1ac63c091e42faaedc4dc307529a1a668111a3c6a6ad3bcc0.png)\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2.\n$$\n\nThe circular piece contributes\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac12(2\\pi\\cdot 7)=7 \\pi.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula for the straight pieces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(7-(-7))^2+(1-(1))^2}=14.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd every traversed length positively:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7 \\pi+14=14 + 7 \\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719141 :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff6a09145d9dc2ac55a5c8733739f90745c8153e6c163e58c5e9494566542427.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ follows the three-quarter-circle arc and then the horizontal segment, once in the direction of the arrows. The circular arc has radius $5$ and center $(0,0)$. The straight part joins $A(0,-5)$, $B(5,-5)$." :question/worked-solution "The **line integral of unity** is the total path length. Split the path into its 2 pieces:\n\n![](images/5b/5bb369ff483e884491e094ec520e74f8f3deebb970a21531b0c779d8e93b6f2f.png)\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2.\n$$\n\nThe circular piece contributes\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac34(2\\pi\\cdot 5)=\\frac{15 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula for the straight pieces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(5-(0))^2+(-5-(-5))^2}=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd every traversed length positively:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{15 \\pi}{2}+5=5 + \\frac{15 \\pi}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719152 :question/problem "![](images/37/37d5d0a6b1ca2b4e3885b6bbe723f6826cead5a0ef7eb5277161c685d7ccbd05.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ follows the three-quarter-circle arc and then the horizontal segment, once in the direction of the arrows. The circular arc has radius $1$ and center $(0,0)$. The straight part joins $A(0,-1)$, $B(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "The **line integral of unity** is the total path length. Split the path into its 2 pieces:\n\n![](images/c5/c57aee3e62e79d5c74c2c0e01e70d2a54f45e54cf535f73c10a77e6e7c1017d2.png)\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2.\n$$\n\nThe circular piece contributes\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac34(2\\pi\\cdot 1)=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula for the straight pieces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(1-(0))^2+(-1-(-1))^2}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd every traversed length positively:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}+1=1 + \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719155 :question/problem "![](images/88/88063c9ad935a0f48653c9fefded6ed35ea8168956a76c5a9c523da6ac467185.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ follows the semicircular arc and then its diameter, once in the direction of the arrows. The circular arc has radius $3$ and center $(0,1)$. The straight part joins $A(-3,1)$, $B(3,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The **line integral of unity** is the total path length. Split the path into its 2 pieces:\n\n![](images/a2/a29e3eb1db737d61a8a69047ccfac6ada966d4bf11d2b8aebf87071632480cd9.png)\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2.\n$$\n\nThe circular piece contributes\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac12(2\\pi\\cdot 3)=3 \\pi.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula for the straight pieces:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(3-(-3))^2+(1-(1))^2}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd every traversed length positively:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=3 \\pi+6=6 + 3 \\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719165 :question/problem "The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(t)=\\langle 2(t)^4-(t)^2,(t)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le 2$. The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(u)=\\langle 2(1-3u)^4-(1-3u)^2,(1-3u)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{3}$. Suppose $f$ is continuous along both curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-7.\n$$\n\nA minus sign on a curve means its orientation is reversed. **Which statements must be true** for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write points on the known curve in terms of a geometric parameter $z$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(z)=\\langle 2(z)^4-(z)^2,(z)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $C_1$, $z=t$ increases from $0$ to $2$. For $C_2$, $z=1-3u$ decreases from $1$ to $0$. Eliminating the parameter on $C_1$ gives $x=2y^4-y^2$. The other curve covers only the part with $0\\le z\\le 1$, rather than $0\\le z\\le 2$. A shared Cartesian equation alone is insufficient. A continuous function supported near an interior point of the omitted arc can be scaled to give any prescribed nonzero integral on the full curve, while its integral on the subarc stays zero. Thus no nonzero fixed multiple of the full integral is guaranteed on the subarc. \n\nReversing $C_1$ always preserves its value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-7.\n$$\n\nChanging the parameter speed does not supply a multiplicative factor. \n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-7$, is **true**. Statement 2, $\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-7$, is **not guaranteed**. Statement 3, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-\\frac{7}{3}$, is **not guaranteed**. "}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719184 :question/problem "The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(t)=\\langle (t),2(t)^{3/2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le 2$. The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(u)=\\langle (1-2u),2(1-2u)^{3/2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}$. Suppose $f$ is continuous along both curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nA minus sign on a curve means its orientation is reversed. **Which statements must be true** for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write points on the known curve in terms of a geometric parameter $z$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(z)=\\langle (z),2(z)^{3/2}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $C_1$, $z=t$ increases from $0$ to $2$. For $C_2$, $z=1-2u$ decreases from $1$ to $0$. Eliminating the parameter on $C_1$ gives $y=2x^{3/2}$. The other curve covers only the part with $0\\le z\\le 1$, rather than $0\\le z\\le 2$. A shared Cartesian equation alone is insufficient. A continuous function supported near an interior point of the omitted arc can be scaled to give any prescribed nonzero integral on the full curve, while its integral on the subarc stays zero. Thus no nonzero fixed multiple of the full integral is guaranteed on the subarc. \n\nReversing $C_1$ always preserves its value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nChanging the parameter speed does not supply a multiplicative factor. \n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=3$, is **true**. Statement 2, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=6$, is **not guaranteed**. Statement 3, $\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=3$, is **not guaranteed**. "}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719187 :question/problem "The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(t)=\\langle 2(t)^4-(t)^2,(t)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le 1$. The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(u)=\\langle 2(3u)^4-(3u)^2,(3u)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{3}$. Suppose $f$ is continuous along both curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nA minus sign on a curve means its orientation is reversed. **Which statements must be true** for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write points on the known curve in terms of a geometric parameter $z$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(z)=\\langle 2(z)^4-(z)^2,(z)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $C_2$, $z=t$ increases from $0$ to $1$. For $C_1$, $z=3u$ increases from $0$ to $1$. Eliminating the parameter on $C_2$ gives $x=2y^4-y^2$. The other curve has the same locus, branch and full parameter range; both traverse the arc once. The parameter map is $t=(3u)/1$. Its sign controls direction, not the scalar integral. \n\nReversing $C_2$ always preserves its value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nIndependence of parametrization and orientation also gives $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$. Changing the parameter speed does not supply a multiplicative factor. \n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-6$, is **not guaranteed**. Statement 2, $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$, is **true**. Statement 3, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$, is **true**. "}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719188 :question/problem "The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(t)=\\langle 3\\cos(t),3\\sin(t)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le \\pi$. The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(u)=\\langle 3\\cos(\\pi-3u),3\\sin(\\pi-3u)\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{3}\\pi$. Suppose $f$ is continuous along both curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nA minus sign on a curve means its orientation is reversed. **Which statements must be true** for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write points on the known curve in terms of a geometric parameter $z$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(z)=\\langle 3\\cos(z),3\\sin(z)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $C_2$, $z=t$ increases from $0$ to $\\pi$. For $C_1$, $z=\\pi-3u$ decreases from $\\pi$ to $0$. Eliminating the parameter on $C_2$ gives $x^2+y^2=9,\\quad y\\ge0$. The other curve has the same locus, branch and full parameter range; both traverse the arc once. The parameter map is $t=(\\pi-3u)/1$. Its sign controls direction, not the scalar integral. \n\nReversing $C_2$ always preserves its value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nIndependence of parametrization and orientation also gives $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$. Changing the parameter speed does not supply a multiplicative factor. \n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-6$, is **not guaranteed**. Statement 2, $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$, is **true**. Statement 3, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-2$, is **true**. "}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719192 :question/problem "The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(t)=\\langle (t),3(t)^{3/2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le 2$. The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(u)=\\langle (3u),3(3u)^{3/2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac{2}{3}$. Suppose $f$ is continuous along both curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nA minus sign on a curve means its orientation is reversed. **Which statements must be true** for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write points on the known curve in terms of a geometric parameter $z$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(z)=\\langle (z),3(z)^{3/2}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor $C_2$, $z=t$ increases from $0$ to $2$. For $C_1$, $z=3u$ increases from $0$ to $2$. Eliminating the parameter on $C_2$ gives $y=3x^{3/2}$. The other curve has the same locus, branch and full parameter range; both traverse the arc once. The parameter map is $t=(3u)/1$. Its sign controls direction, not the scalar integral. \n\nReversing $C_2$ always preserves its value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nIndependence of parametrization and orientation also gives $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8$. Changing the parameter speed does not supply a multiplicative factor. \n\nStatement 1, $\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8$, is **true**. Statement 2, $\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8$, is **true**. Statement 3, $\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{8}{3}$, is **not guaranteed**. "}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719262 :question/problem "The paths $C$ and $D$ trace exactly the same geometric arc once, in the same direction. The path $D$ follows $C$ in the same direction.\n\nThe parametrization of $D$ runs 2 times as fast, with its parameter interval shortened accordingly. Suppose $f$ is continuous along the arc and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$?" :question/worked-solution "Scalar line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization and orientation. Both paths cover the same arc once, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nThe speed change is offset by the shortened parameter interval. Reversing direction does not negate the integral, and a single traversal does not cancel it. Neither multiplying nor dividing by the parameter speed is appropriate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719263 :question/problem "The paths $C$ and $D$ trace exactly the same geometric arc once, in opposite directions. The path $D=-C$ is the reversed path in the diagram.\n\n![The same curve traversed in opposite directions.](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nThe parametrization of $D$ runs 2 times as fast, with its parameter interval shortened accordingly. Suppose $f$ is continuous along the arc and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$?" :question/worked-solution "Scalar line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization and orientation. Both paths cover the same arc once, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=4.\n$$\n\nThe speed change is offset by the shortened parameter interval. Reversing direction does not negate the integral, and a single traversal does not cancel it. Neither multiplying nor dividing by the parameter speed is appropriate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719264 :question/problem "The paths $C$ and $D$ trace exactly the same geometric arc once, in the same direction. The path $D$ follows $C$ in the same direction.\n\nThe parametrization of $D$ runs 3 times as fast, with its parameter interval shortened accordingly. Suppose $f$ is continuous along the arc and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$?" :question/worked-solution "Scalar line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization and orientation. Both paths cover the same arc once, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nThe speed change is offset by the shortened parameter interval. Reversing direction does not negate the integral, and a single traversal does not cancel it. Neither multiplying nor dividing by the parameter speed is appropriate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719266 :question/problem "The paths $C$ and $D$ trace exactly the same geometric arc once, in opposite directions. The path $D=-C$ is the reversed path in the diagram.\n\n![The same curve traversed in opposite directions.](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nThe parametrization of $D$ runs 3 times as fast, with its parameter interval shortened accordingly. Suppose $f$ is continuous along the arc and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$?" :question/worked-solution "Scalar line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization and orientation. Both paths cover the same arc once, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{D} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nThe speed change is offset by the shortened parameter interval. Reversing direction does not negate the integral, and a single traversal does not cancel it. Neither multiplying nor dividing by the parameter speed is appropriate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719284 :question/problem "![Curve and its measurements](images/26/26ebfac5fbca51116e97437adee9e3c64f41a5055e6398bd3b594e3992e72517.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment shown, traversed once from $B$ to $A$." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints are $A(2,-1)$ and $B(-6,-1)$. The segment is horizontal. Its endpoint distance is\n\n$$\nd=AB=|-6-(2)|=8.\n$$\n\nHere the path is the segment itself, so\n\n$$\nL=d=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nReversing the direction leaves traveled length unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719292 :question/problem "![Curve and its measurements](images/d0/d0bb8c5625964fb0945e00cccf2e382dc69f5cb6fe40e2dea257fdb2191d7c8b.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle with radius $5$ shown, traversed once clockwise. The dashed guide is not part of the path." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe supplied radius determines the diameter:\n\n$$\nd=2r=2(5)=10.\n$$\n\nThe path is a full circle. Its circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\pi d\\\\&=\\pi(10)=10 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=10 \\pi.\n$$\n\nReversing the direction leaves traveled length unchanged. A closed path has zero net displacement, but its length is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719298 :question/problem "![Curve and its measurements](images/52/52af9feefbf91f7b3f61290a39b5e421ef13c4cbe4f5d0528a51126dd939bb0b.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment shown, traversed once from $A$ to $B$." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints are $A(0,-3)$ and $B(0,-10)$. The segment is vertical. Its endpoint distance is\n\n$$\nd=AB=|-10-(-3)|=7.\n$$\n\nHere the path is the segment itself, so\n\n$$\nL=d=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s=7.\n$$\n\nReversing the direction leaves traveled length unchanged."}]}
